 "narrative": [
  "## What this business physically is\n**A paper mill buys waste paper and pulp, burns power and coal to cook and dry it, and sells reels or sheets by the tonne.** Cash comes in per tonne sold; cost sits in raw material, power and steam, so the spread between input cost and realisation decides everything. Kuantum's own numbers show the direction of travel: a double displacement digester lifted pulp yield by 1% and cut steam from 1.8 to 0.8 metric tons per ton of pulp [4] — **efficiency, not price, is where a mill makes its money now.**\n\n## Where cash actually sits\n**Working capital is the real exposure, not the machine.** A rated peer's file shows the pattern plainly: full advance to suppliers, 45-60 days to recover from customers, funded by working capital bank lines [8]. So when a proposal lands, check the advance-to-supplier percentage and the debtor days first; if the buyer is a trader rather than an end user, ask for the last six months of ledger and the top five debtor ageing. Rating commentary also flags wastewater treatment as a penalty and shutdown risk [10] — **ask for the consent-to-operate validity date on every proposal.**\n\n## What has changed now\n**The local demand picture is weak and the national output picture is flat.** Uttarakhand GST collections are -19.1% Apr-to-date against India +6.1%, 25.2 pp slower [D], and state electricity demand is +4.7% against India +9.5%, 4.8 pp slower [D]. India-wide IIP for NIC 17 is -0.1% YoY to Aug-2026 [D], though +8.6% Apr-to-date [D]. Note the local sources are thin and dated — [1] is April 2026, [3] is November 2024 — **we are leaning on first principles and peers here, not on Kashipur-specific data.**\n\n## Who to pursue, and how to grow safely\n**Pursue converters and box/board units with end-user order books, not mills adding capacity.** ICRA notes significant capacity addition in the last two years with the pace moderating in FY2026, and margins exposed to cheaper imports and volatile realisations [11]. So when a proposal lands, check whether the borrower is adding tonnage into a flat market; if yes, ask why and decline unless the offtake is contracted. ## Early warning signs\n**Watch the input side and the local demand side together.** Take GST returns monthly and compare the borrower's own trend against the state's -19.1% [D]; a borrower falling faster than the state is the first sign. Watch electricity consumption against the state's +4.7% [D] — a mill running below trend is losing orders. Any insurance claim or stock write-off after a yard fire [1] needs the surveyor's report before the next renewal."
 ],
